  <content>
  <section><para>In this lesson, you will use graphs to learn about the domain of functions. You will examine and interpret different types of graphs that represent real-world situations to determine the domain and range.</para>
<para>When you finish this lesson, you will be able to:</para>
<list>
  <item> Determine a reasonable domain and range for the function when given a description of a function in a situation. </item>
</list>
<para>Here are the<emphasis> activities </emphasis>that will help you reach those goals:</para>
<list>
  <item> 4.13.1: Different Features of a Graph </item>
  <item> 4.13.2: Graphs of Functions </item>
  <list>
    <item> 4.13.2: Self Check </item>
    <item> 4.13.2: Additional Resources </item>
  </list>
  <item> 4.13.3: Finding Domain and Range Using a Graph </item>
  <list>
    <item> 4.13.3: Self Check </item>
    <item> 4.13.3: Additional Resources </item>
  </list>
  <item> 4.13.4: Real-World Domain and Range </item>
  <list>
    <item> 4.13.4: Self Check </item>
    <item> 4.13.4: Additional Resources </item>
  </list>
  <item> 4.13.5: Graph of a Function and the Domain and Range Connection </item>
</list>
<para>After that, you’ll<emphasis> practice and review</emphasis>.</para>
<list>
  <item> 4.13.6: Practice </item>
  <item> 4.13.7: Lesson Summary </item>
</list></section>
</content>
